Here is an independent method claim tailored for a patent application on non-intrusive load monitoring (NILM):

**1.** A computer-implemented method for non-intrusive load monitoring, comprising:
> receiving, by one or more processors, utility meter data comprising time-series energy consumption measurements associated with a target facility;
> 
> detecting, by the one or more processors, positive consumption variations and negative consumption variations within the received utility meter data;
> 
> matching, by the one or more processors, a detected positive consumption variation with a corresponding negative consumption variation to identify a paired device turn-on event and device turn-off event;
> 
> grouping, by the one or more processors, a plurality of identified paired turn-on events and turn-off events into one or more usage blocks based at least in part on temporal characteristics or power signatures of the paired events;
> 
> classifying, by the one or more processors, the one or more usage blocks into one or more predetermined clusters associated with known device profiles; and
> 
> detecting operational usage of at least one specific electrical device at the target facility based on the classification of the one or more usage blocks.

A computer-implemented method for disaggregating utility consumption data to determine electrical device operation, the method comprising:
receiving, by at least one processor, a time-series stream of meter data representing electrical consumption of a monitored environment;
detecting, by the at least one processor within the time-series stream of meter data, a plurality of consumption deltas corresponding to activation and deactivation power state transitions;
matching, by the at least one processor, positive power variations among the detected consumption deltas with corresponding negative power variations based on magnitude and temporal proximity parameters;
forming, by the at least one processor, a plurality of usage blocks, wherein each usage block is temporally bounded by a matched pair of a positive power variation and a negative power variation;
classifying, by the at least one processor, each of the plurality of usage blocks into one of a plurality of pre-defined appliance clusters based on operational signature features derived therefrom; and
outputting, via an electronic communication interface or graphical user interface, a device usage status corresponding to at least one electrical device identified by the classification of the usage blocks.
